## Borrowing Without Giving Up Ownership

Use `&` to create a read-only reference. The original owner keeps the value:

```xiom
fn read_vec(v: &Vec[Int]) {
  io.println(v.len());
  // Can read, can't modify
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let my_vec = Vec[Int].new();
  my_vec.push(10);
  my_vec.push(20);
  read_vec(&my_vec);    // Borrow — my_vec is still valid!
  io.println(my_vec.len()); // Works! my_vec was never moved
  return my_vec.len() as Int;
}
```

### Multiple Read Borrows Are Allowed

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let data = Vec[Int].new();
  data.push(1);
  data.push(2);

  let r1 = &data;  // First read borrow
  let r2 = &data;  // Second read borrow — OK!
  let r3 = &data;  // Third read borrow — also OK!

  io.println(r1.len());
  io.println(r2.len());
  io.println(r3.len());
  return data.len() as Int;
}
```

### Read Borrows Are the Default Pattern

Use `&` as your default way to pass data to functions. Only use owned parameters when the function truly needs to take ownership, and `&mut` when it needs to modify.

